{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2299 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "cb3d1f1ba6bde81b337281ec7584025c3e36e8f7874966587ad7c510fa79fa4a" :edb/db-before-t 2298 :edb/db-after-t 2299 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535611 50 #inst "2026-10-09T03:36:00.776Z" 13194139535611 true] [17592186166499 1100 "Let $F(x)=x^2+10x$ for $[MATH: x\\in[0,6),]$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in (-6,6)$. What is the value of $f(-1)$?" 13194139535611 false] [17592186166499 1100 "Let $F(x)=x^2+10x$ for $x\\in[0,6),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in (-6,6).$ What is the value of $f(-1)?$" 13194139535611 true] [17592186166499 1145 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $[MATH: x\\in[0,6),]$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n$[MATH: {F(x), \\mid x ∈ [0, 6),; - F(-x), \\mid x ∈ (-6, 0).]$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in(-6,0)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= -F(-x) \\\\\n&= - ((-x)^{2} + 10(-x)) \\\\\n&= - (x^{2} - 10x) \\\\\n&=-x^{2} + 10x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-1) &= - (-1)^{2} + 10(-1) =-11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535611 false] [17592186166499 1145 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in[0,6),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068497/assets/3d7a4a25652434612765ebef26c7c0956342611968008bc4908a4865297e047d.png)\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in(-6,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}+10(-x)) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-10x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-(-1)^{2}+10(-1)=-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535611 true] [17592186166500 1100 "Let $F(x)=4x$ for $[MATH: x\\in[0,\\pi).]$ Find the missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$AAAAA_{A}^{A}$" 13194139535611 false] [17592186166500 1100 "Let $F(x)=4x$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ Find the missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068497/assets/20ec903a94cb720f0d2c50b427409e36705af1f9f226b5c6fb11f93f92f0dd49.png)" 13194139535611 true] [17592186166500 1145 "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\nb_n=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi f(x)\\sin(nx)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nf(x)=4x\n$$\n\nfor $x \\in (0,\\pi)$. So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\nb_n = \\dfrac{8}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi x \\sin(nx)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u = x & ⇒u^{′} = 1 \\\\ v^{′} = \\sin (nx) & ⇒v =-\\frac{1}{n}\\cos (nx)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb_{n} &= \\left.\\frac{8}{π}(uv\\right|_{0}^{π} - \\int_{0}^{π}vu^{′}dx) \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{π}(-\\frac{1}{n}[x\\cos (nx)]_{0}^{π} + \\frac{1}{n}\\int_{0}^{π}\\cos (nx)dx) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{8}{nπ}((π \\cdot \\cos (nπ) - 0 \\cdot \\cos (0)) - \\frac{1}{n}[\\sin (nx)]_{0}^{π}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{8}{nπ}(π\\cos (nπ) - \\frac{1}{n}(\\sin (nπ) - \\sin (0))) \\\\\n&= - (8\\cos (nπ))/(n)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\n\\cos(n\\pi)=(-1)^n\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nb_n = -\\dfrac{8(-1)^n}{n}=\\dfrac{8(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier sine series is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{8(-1)^{n+1}}{n} \\cdot \\sin(nx)\n$$" 13194139535611 false] [17592186166500 1145 "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\nb_n=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi f(x)\\sin(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=4x$ for $x \\in (0,\\pi).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\nb_n = \\dfrac{8}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi x \\sin(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{n\\pi }(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\cos(n\\pi)=(-1)^n,$ we have\n\n$$\nb_n = -\\dfrac{8(-1)^n}{n}=\\dfrac{8(-1)^{n+1}}{n}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier sine series is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{8(-1)^{n+1}}{n} \\cdot \\sin(nx).\n$$" 13194139535611 true] [17592186166887 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357452 13194139535611 true] [17592186166887 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357459 13194139535611 true] [17592186166887 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357466 13194139535611 true] [17592186166887 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357491 13194139535611 true] [17592186166887 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357511 13194139535611 true] [17592186166888 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357473 13194139535611 true] [17592186166888 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357484 13194139535611 true] [17592186166888 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357494 13194139535611 true] [17592186166888 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357505 13194139535611 true] [17592186167221 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535611 true] [17592186167221 1100 "Let $F(x) =-5x$ for $[MATH: x ∈ [0, π).]$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2π$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.$(2(-1)^{n})/(n)$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" 13194139535611 false] [17592186167221 1100 "Let $F(x)=-5x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" 13194139535611 true] [17592186167221 1145 "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-5x$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{10}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{10}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{n\\pi }(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier sine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$" 13194139535611 true] [17592186357452 1017 #edb/ref 17592186357453 13194139535611 true] [17592186357452 1089 "q-340866" 13194139535611 true] [17592186357452 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535611 true] [17592186357452 1100 "Let $F(x)=3{x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [0,5),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-5,5).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-5,0)?$" 13194139535611 true